# The ADR-014 interop vectors are NORMATIVE test data (ADR-014 §4). They are compared
# byte-for-byte against h2a-protocol and bridle by scripts/check-vectors-pinned.mjs, and pinned
# by a committed SHA-256 — so their bytes must not depend on who checked them out.
#
# This file exists because that check failed on its first run. hdicr had no .gitattributes, so a
# Windows checkout with core.autocrlf converted 124 line endings and the fixture became 9497 bytes
# instead of 9373. The CONTENT was identical — strip the CRs and the digest matched exactly — but
# the bytes were not, and "byte-identical across three repositories" was therefore false on
# Windows while CI, which runs on Linux, stayed green. A check whose result depends on the
# platform it runs on tests the machine, not the repository. That is the same defect class as the
# missing `pnpm build` step this workflow gained on 2026-08-07.
#
# `-text` disables conversion outright rather than normalising, so these bytes are stable by
# construction rather than by configuration — a stray `git config core.autocrlf` on one
# contributor's machine cannot reintroduce it. Mirrors h2a-protocol's protection of the same
# files upstream.
services/credentials-service/test/fixtures/**   -text

# The pin script itself, for the same reason and it is easy to miss. Its stated design property is
# that it is BYTE-IDENTICAL in h2a-protocol, bridle and hdicr — "the file differing between
# repositories is itself the signal". A CRLF checkout here makes that claim false on Windows while
# leaving it true on Linux, which is precisely the kind of quietly-platform-dependent truth this
# script exists to eliminate. Git warned about this while the fixture fix was being staged.
scripts/check-vectors-pinned.mjs                -text
